Found it — clear and confirmed by both source and tests.

**Behavior: (a) drops the OLDEST buffered sample, accepts the newest.**

`/work/src/ingest.ts` — `IngestQueue` class:
- Fields: `buffer: Sample[]`, `dropped = 0`, `capacity` (lines 39-41)
- `push()` (lines 50-56):
```
push(sample: Sample): void {
  this.buffer.push(sample);
  while (this.buffer.length > this.capacity) {
    this.buffer.shift();
    this.dropped += 1;
  }
}
```
  The new sample is always appended (`buffer.push`), then if capacity is exceeded, the front of the array is removed via `buffer.shift()` — i.e., the oldest entry — incrementing `dropped`.
- Doc comment at lines 34-36 states this explicitly: "Overflow drops the *oldest* sample, not the newest: under sustained pressure a recent picture beats a stale one."
- Dropped-counter metric: `dropped` field (line 40), exposed via `droppedCount()` at lines 70-72 ("How many samples overflow has discarded since construction").

Test confirmation in `/work/test/ingest.test.ts`, lines 47-57 (`"drops the oldest sample on overflow"`): pushing values `[1, 2, 3]` into a capacity-2 queue and draining yields `[2, 3]` (value `1`, the oldest, was evicted), with `droppedCount()` equal to `1`.

Both `IngestQueue` references in `/work/src/router.ts` and `/work/src/index.ts` are just call sites (construction/usage), not additional logic — the eviction policy lives entirely in `ingest.ts`.
